[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:The Process of Ageing]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.73]Lesson 37 | 第37课
[00:03.12]The process of ageing | 衰老过程
[00:11.66]What is one of the most unpleasant discoveries we make about ourselves as we get older? | 随着年龄的增长，我们对自己最不愉快的发现之一是什么？
[00:20.55]At the age of twelve years, the human body is at its most vigorous. | 人在12岁时身体最精力充沛。
[00:26.85]It has yet to reach its full size and strength, and its owner his or her full intelligence: but at this age the likelihood of death is least. | 这时身体尚未达到完全的身高和力量，其主人也尚未达到智力的顶峰：但在这个年龄死亡的可能性最小。
[00:39.39]Earlier, we were infants and young children, and consequently more vulnerable; | 在这之前，我们是婴儿和幼儿，因而更为脆弱；
[00:47.25]later, we shall undergo a progressive loss of our vigour and resistance which, though imperceptible at first | 在这以后，我们将经历精力和抵抗力的逐渐丧失，这种丧失起初难以察觉，
[00:56.61]will finally become so steep that we can live no longer, | 但最终会变得如此急剧，以至于我们再也无法活下去，
[01:01.71]however well we look after ourselves, and however well society, and our doctors, look after us. | 无论我们把自己照顾得多么好，也无论社会和医生把我们照顾得多么好。
[01:10.10]This decline in vigour with the passing of time is called ageing. | 这种随着时间流逝而精力衰退的现象叫做衰老。
[01:15.34]It is one of the most unpleasant discoveries which we all make that we must decline in this way, that if we escape wars, | 它是我们所有人都会有的最不愉快的发现之一：我们必定会这样衰老，即使我们逃脱了战争、
[01:25.35]accidents and diseases we shall eventually 'die of old age', and that this happens at a rate which differs little from person to person, | 事故和疾病，我们最终也将“老死”，而且这在人与人之间的差别甚微，
[01:36.90]so that there are heavy odds in favour of our dying between the ages of 65 and 80. | 因此我们极有可能在65岁至80岁之间死去。
[01:44.68]Some of us will die sooner, a few will live longer -- on into a ninth or tenth decade. | 有些人会死得早一些，少数人会活得长一些——活到九旬或十旬。
[01:52.89]But the chances are against it, | 但机会是不利的，
[01:55.45]and there is a virtual limit on how long we can hope to remain alive, however lucky and robust we are. | 而且无论我们多么幸运、多么健壮，我们能希望活下去的时间实际上有个极限。
[02:03.88]Normal people tend to forget this process unless and until they are reminded of it. | 正常人往往忘记这一过程，除非有人提醒他们。
[02:10.41]We are so familiar with the fact that man ages, that people have for years assumed that the process of losing vigour with time, | 我们对人会衰老这一事实太熟悉了，以至于人们多年来一直认为，随着时间流逝而丧失精力、
[02:19.48]of becoming more likely to die the older we get, was something self-evident | 年龄越大越可能死亡的过程，是某种不言自明的事情，
[02:25.37]like the cooling of a hot kettle or the wearing-out of a pair of shoes. | 就像热水壶的冷却或一双鞋子的穿破一样。
[02:30.73]They have also assumed that all animals, | 他们还假定，一切动物，
[02:34.17]and probably other organisms such as trees, or even the universe itself, must in the nature of things 'wear out'. | 以及大概其他生物，比如树木，甚至宇宙本身，都必然会在事物本性中“磨损掉”。
[02:44.82]Most animals we commonly observe do in fact age as we do, | 我们通常观察到的大多数动物确实像我们一样衰老，
[02:49.82]if given the chance to live long enough; | 如果让它们活得足够长的话；
[02:52.42]and mechanical systems like a wound watch, or the sun, | 而像上紧发条的表或太阳这样的机械系统，
[02:57.54]do in fact run out of energy in accordance with the second law of thermodynamics (whether the whole universe does so is a moot point at present). | 确实按照热力学第二定律耗尽了能量（整个宇宙是否如此目前尚无定论）。
[03:09.17]But these are not analogous to what happens when man ages. | 但这些都与人类衰老时所发生的情况不相似。
[03:14.68]A run-down watch is still a watch and can be rewound. | 一只停了的表仍是表，还能重新上弦。
[03:19.89]An old watch, by contrast, becomes so worn and unreliable that it eventually is not worth mending. | 而一块旧表则磨损得如此厉害、如此不可靠，以至于最终不值得修理。
[03:29.98]But a watch could never repair itself--it does not consist of living parts, only of metal, which wears away by friction. | 但表决不能自我修理——它不由活的部件组成，只由会因摩擦而磨损的金属组成。
[03:40.98]We could, at one time, repair ourselves--well enough, at least, to overcome all but the most instantly fatal illnesses and accidents. | 我们曾在某个时期自我修理——至少足以战胜除了最致命的疾病和事故以外的一切。
[03:53.69]Between twelve and eighty years we gradually lose this power; | 在12岁到80岁之间，我们逐渐失去了这种能力；
[03:59.72]an illness which at 12 would knock us over, at 80 can knock us out, and into our grave. | 12岁时只会把我们击倒的疾病，80岁时能把我们击垮，并送进坟墓。
[04:08.79]If we could stay as vigorous as we are at twelve, | 假如我们能像12岁时那样保持精力充沛，
[04:12.54]it would take about 700 years for half of us to die, and another 700 for the survivors to be reduced by half again. | 大约要过700年才会有一半人死亡，再过700年，幸存者又会减少一半。
